WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.563
HNSW: a navigable graph with express lanes.

2
00:00:03.843 --> 00:00:14.552
The ANN method you are most likely to meet first is HNSW, Hierarchical Navigable Small World graphs, from Malkov and Yashunin (2016).

3
00:00:14.832 --> 00:00:19.419
Faiss and pgvector both implement it, as do many vector databases.

4
00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:21.021
Start with one layer.

5
00:00:21.301 --> 00:00:25.312
Connect every vector to a handful of its near neighbors, making a graph.

6
00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:35.640
To search, begin at an entry point and walk greedily: look at the current node’s neighbors, move to whichever is closest to the query, and stop when no neighbor is closer.

7
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.765
On a well-built graph this reaches the query’s neighborhood after visiting a small fraction of the nodes.

8
00:00:42.045 --> 00:00:47.592
The “small world” property, short paths between any two nodes, is what makes greedy routing work.

9
00:00:47.872 --> 00:00:52.651
A single layer has a problem: early in the search, the walk takes many short steps.

10
00:00:52.931 --> 00:00:58.435
HNSW adds a hierarchy, much like a skip list (the paper draws this analogy).

11
00:00:58.715 --> 00:01:11.344
Each vector is assigned a maximum layer at random, with exponentially decreasing probability for higher layers, so the top layer has very few nodes with long-range links, and each layer below is denser.

12
00:01:11.624 --> 00:01:21.032
Search starts at the top, takes a few long hops to get close, then drops down a layer and refines, until it reaches the bottom layer that contains every vector.

13
00:01:21.312 --> 00:01:31.403
The paper reports logarithmic scaling of search complexity with this structure, and a neighbor-selection heuristic that helps most at high recall and on clustered data.

14
00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:40.643
Three parameters (named as in Faiss; vector databases use similar names) control the trade-offs: M: how many neighbors each node keeps.

15
00:01:40.923 --> 00:01:43.696
Higher M means better recall and more memory.

16
00:01:43.976 --> 00:01:48.093
efConstruction: how widely the builder searches when inserting a vector.

17
00:01:48.373 --> 00:01:50.763
Higher means a better graph and slower builds.

18
00:01:51.043 --> 00:01:53.965
efSearch: how many candidates the search keeps in play.

19
00:01:54.245 --> 00:01:59.600
This is the dial you tune at query time: higher means better recall and slower queries.

20
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.181
Know the costs, too.

21
00:02:01.461 --> 00:02:16.245
Faiss estimates HNSW memory at roughly d times 4 plus M times 8 bytes per vector (the full vector plus its links), so it doesn’t compress anything, and its HNSW index doesn’t support removing vectors.

22
00:02:16.525 --> 00:02:20.344
Databases that support deletes handle it with extra machinery.
